WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.840
This is the T cell receptor.

2
00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:10.160
It tells a T cell that it has found an infected cell.

3
00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:15.560
But inside the cell, its tail is only a few amino acids long.

4
00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:19.800
It holds no enzyme anywhere, not even a kinase.

5
00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:22.300
So how does it send a message?

6
00:00:23.333 --> 00:00:26.733
The alpha and beta chains do the recognising.

7
00:00:27.613 --> 00:00:31.853
Beside them sit partner chains, called CD3 and zeta.

8
00:00:32.753 --> 00:00:34.733
Their tails reach into the cell.

9
00:00:35.673 --> 00:00:41.273
Each tail carries a short motif of two tyrosines, a fixed short distance apart.

10
00:00:42.213 --> 00:00:44.473
This motif is called an ITAM.

11
00:00:45.367 --> 00:00:47.427
So where is the kinase?

12
00:00:48.067 --> 00:00:49.527
It is borrowed from a neighbour.

13
00:00:50.487 --> 00:00:58.047
A co-receptor, CD4 or CD8, grips the display protein MHC that the receptor reads.

14
00:00:58.967 --> 00:01:05.027
A share of the tyrosine kinase Lck rides on its inner tail, beside the partner

15
00:01:05.027 --> 00:01:09.787
tails. Lck then adds a phosphate to both tyrosines.

16
00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:15.400
Now the reader arrives: ZAP-70, a second kinase.

17
00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:19.580
It has two SH2 domains, side by side.

18
00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:26.520
Each domain grips one phosphotyrosine, and the pair is spaced to fit the two on one

19
00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:30.280
tail. With one phosphate, it binds weakly.

20
00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:36.660
It binds tightly only when both tyrosines carry a phosphate, so one stray phosphate

21
00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:38.140
does not start a T cell.

22
00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:45.580
Docking opens ZAP-70, which phosphorylates LAT, a scaffold set in the membrane.

23
00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:52.280
From LAT, one branch raises calcium, and NFAT enters the nucleus.

24
00:01:53.480 --> 00:02:01.820
A second runs through DAG and protein kinase C to NF-κB. A third loads Ras, and

25
00:02:01.820 --> 00:02:04.060
ERK helps build AP-1.

26
00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:08.640
The three meet on genes such as the one for interleukin-2.

27
00:02:09.450 --> 00:02:15.950
Even this is not enough, because receptors vary almost at random, and some fit the

28
00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:17.250
body's own peptides.

29
00:02:18.050 --> 00:02:23.470
In classic experiments on cultured T cells, signal one alone did worse than nothing.

30
00:02:24.190 --> 00:02:28.090
The cells entered a lasting unresponsive state called anergy.

31
00:02:28.910 --> 00:02:35.050
A second signal needs the receptor CD28 to bind a B7 protein on the presenting cell.

32
00:02:35.050 --> 00:02:40.010
Keep the rule: a T cell needs two phosphates and two signals.
